<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Molière</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1622">1622</a>–<a href="/years/1673">1673</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Jean-Baptiste Poquelin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Molière, the stage name of Jean-Baptiste Poquelin, was a French playwright and actor, regarded as the greatest master of French comedy. Supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, his company performed at court and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. His plays, among them Tartuffe, The Misanthrope, The Miser and The Imaginary Invalid, satirised hypocrisy, pretension and the professions, and Tartuffe was banned for several years after protests by the clergy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moliere.jpg" alt="Molière" width="320" height="363" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pierre Mignard I · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_Mignard_-_Portrait_de_Jean-Baptiste_Poquelin_dit_Moli%C3%A8re_(1622-1673)_-_Google_Art_Project_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the seventeenth century Descartes, Corneille, <b>Molière</b> and Racine, the Académie française founded in 1635 and the court of Versailles made French taste the standard of European elites, and French increasingly replaced Latin as the language of diplomacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The first play in Arabic, Marun al-Naqqash’s al-Bakhil (The Miser), adapted from <b>Molière</b>, was performed in Beirut in 1847; by 1850 he had built a theatre near his home for a new play drawn from the Thousand and One Nights.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis XIV began 'The Pleasures of the Enchanted Island', a week of spectacles and plays including <b>Molière</b>'s first Tartuffe.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-7">7 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1655" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Molière"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1655">What was here in 1655? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;With me you're sure of secrecy, And there's no harm unless a thing is known. The public scandal is what brings offence, And secret sinning is not sin at all.&#8221;</p><footer>Tartuffe (1664), act 4, scene 5 &#183; tr. Curtis Hidden Page, 1908 &#183; spoken by Tartuffe &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2027/pg2027-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Nature, when we leave her free, will herself gently recover from the disorder into which she has fallen. It is our anxiety, our impatience, which does the mischief, and most men die of their remedies, and not of their diseases.&#8221;</p><footer>The Imaginary Invalid (1673), act 3 &#183; tr. Charles Heron Wall, 1879 &#183; spoken by Béralde &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9070/pg9070-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Upon my word, I have been speaking prose these forty years without being aware of it…&#8221;</p><footer>The Shopkeeper Turned Gentleman (1670), act 2 &#183; tr. Charles Heron Wall, 1879 &#183; spoken by Monsieur Jourdain &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7279/pg7279-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ah! How well the man speaks! Come near, let me embrace you for this last saying. It is the finest sentence that I have ever heard in my life: &quot;We must live to eat, and not eat to live.&quot; No; that isn't it.&#8221;</p><footer>The Miser (1668), act 3 &#183; tr. Charles Heron Wall, 1879 &#183; spoken by the miser Harpagon &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6923/pg6923-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
